{%- set faqs = [
  ["What is LLM security testing?", "LLM security testing is a structured assessment of an application built on a large language model, such as a chatbot, copilot or AI assistant. Testers act as an attacker would: they try to override instructions, extract confidential data, trigger unsafe actions and misuse connected tools. The result is a prioritized list of weaknesses with evidence and fixes your team can act on."],
  ["How is LLM security testing different from a normal penetration test?", "A conventional penetration test looks for flaws in code, infrastructure and access controls. An LLM application adds a new layer: the model follows instructions written in natural language, and it cannot reliably tell a developer's instructions from an attacker's. LLM security testing covers that layer, including prompt injection, jailbreaks, system prompt leakage and unsafe tool use, alongside the usual web, API and cloud checks around the model."],
  ["What is prompt injection?", "Prompt injection is an attack in which crafted text persuades a model to ignore its instructions or act against its intended purpose. In a direct attack the user types the malicious instruction. In an indirect attack it is hidden in content the model reads, such as a web page, an email or an uploaded document. Both can lead to data exposure or unauthorized actions when the application trusts the model's output."],
  ["What is the OWASP Top 10 for LLM Applications?", "The OWASP Top 10 for LLM Applications is a community-maintained list of the most critical security risks in applications that use large language models. It includes prompt injection, sensitive information disclosure, supply chain weaknesses, improper output handling, excessive agency, system prompt leakage and unbounded consumption. CipherTrivia can align test coverage and reporting with this list."],
  ["Which LLM applications can you test?", "We can test customer support chatbots, internal copilots, retrieval-augmented generation (RAG) knowledge assistants, code assistants, AI features inside SaaS products and agent-based workflows. Applications built on hosted models from providers such as OpenAI, Anthropic and Google, and on self-hosted open models, can all be assessed."],
  ["Do you need access to the model or its weights?", "No. Most LLM security testing is performed against the application, through its chat interface, APIs and connected tools, in the same way an attacker would reach it. Access to system prompts, architecture diagrams and test accounts is helpful and can make testing deeper, but model weights are not required."],
  ["Can LLM security testing find data leakage?", "Yes. Testing checks whether the application can be made to reveal its system prompt, other users' data, documents it should not retrieve, credentials or internal details. For RAG applications we also check whether retrieval respects the permissions of the person asking."],
  ["Do you test LLM applications that use tools, plugins or function calling?", "Yes. When a model can call tools, query databases or trigger workflows, a successful attack can do more than produce a bad answer. We test what the model is allowed to do, whether those actions are authorized outside the prompt, and whether untrusted content can steer them."],
  ["Will testing affect our production system or increase model costs?", "Testing is planned with your team to limit disruption. It can be run against a staging environment or a production-like copy, and request volumes are agreed in advance so that usage costs and rate limits are not a surprise. Where production testing is required, we agree the scope and timing together."],
  ["How long does LLM security testing take?", "Duration depends on the number of AI features, the connected tools and data sources, and the depth of testing required. A focused assessment of a single chatbot can be completed in a short engagement, while applications with agents and several integrations take longer. We confirm the timeline after scoping."],
  ["How much does LLM security testing cost?", "Cost depends on the application's complexity, the number of integrations, the testing depth and the reporting scope. Share a short description of your application with CipherTrivia and we will recommend a scope and quote."],
  ["What does an LLM security report include?", "The report includes an executive summary, each finding with its severity, the steps to reproduce it, supporting evidence, the affected component and a recommended fix. Findings can be mapped to the OWASP Top 10 for LLM Applications so that engineering, security and compliance teams read them the same way."],
  ["Do you offer retesting after we fix the issues?", "Yes. Retesting can be included to confirm that reported weaknesses have been addressed and that the fixes have not introduced new ones. The retest results are added to the report."],
  ["When should we test an LLM application?", "Test before launch, after changes to prompts, models, tools or data sources, and on a regular schedule once the application is live. Models and prompts change often, and each change can reopen a weakness that was previously closed."]
] %}

