<section class="section section--white" aria-labelledby="s1-title">
  <div class="container">
    <div class="split">
      <div class="split__aside">
        <h2 class="split__title rv" id="s1-title">LLM Security Testing for Chatbots, Copilots and Generative AI Applications</h2>
      </div>
      <div class="split__body prose">
        <p class="rv">Large language models now answer customer questions, summarize internal documents, write code and trigger business workflows. They also follow instructions written in plain language, and they cannot reliably tell an instruction from your developer apart from one hidden in a user message, a web page or an uploaded file. That gap is where most LLM security incidents begin.</p>
        <p class="rv">CipherTrivia's LLM security testing examines the whole application around the model: the system prompt and guardrails, the data the model can retrieve, the tools it can call and the way its output is used. We test it the way an attacker would, using prompt injection, jailbreak techniques, data extraction attempts and abuse of connected systems, and we confirm each issue with reproducible evidence.</p>
        <p class="rv">Whether your application runs on a hosted model from a major provider or on a self-hosted open model, the weaknesses that matter sit in how it is configured, connected and trusted. Our reports show your team exactly where those weaknesses are and how to close them.</p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>

